<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.2.2: Constant and Exponential Change</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01751</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.2.2: Constant and Exponential Change</md:title>
    <md:uuid>9abef150-34c3-4413-810d-4e57485c38d3</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (15 minutes)</title>
<para>In this activity, students contrast two patterns of dots: one pattern shows a linear change, and the other shows a
  quadratic change. They analyze the visual patterns and represent them using tables of values, expressions, and graphs.
</para>
<para>The chosen quadratic pattern enables students to easily see the “squaring” of one quantity: visually, in
  the way the dots are arranged, and numerically, in the values in the table (1, 4, 9, etc.). They also notice that when
  the points representing the relationship are plotted, they form a curve. The term “quadratic” will be
  introduced in the next activity.</para>
<para>Students reason repeatedly about the number of dots at different step numbers and then generalize their observations
  with variable expressions. They look for and make use of structure to extend the visual patterns. If students opt to
  use a spreadsheet or graphing technology, they practice choosing appropriate tools strategically.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<para>Some students may choose to use a spreadsheet tool to study the pattern and subsequently to use graphing technology
  to plot the data. Make these tools accessible, in case they are requested.</para>
<note class="extra-support">
  <title>Support for English Language Learners</title><para>MLR 7 Compare and Connect: Representing, Conversing</para>
  <para>Use this routine to prepare students for the
        whole-class discussion. Give students quiet think time to consider what is the same and what is different about each
        of the pairs of representations (patterns, tables, and graphs). Invite students to discuss what they notice with a
        partner. Listen for and amplify mathematical language students use to describe connections they notice across and
        between the different representations.</para>
      <para><emphasis effect="italics">Design Principle(s): Cultivate conversation</emphasis></para><para>Learn more about this routine</para>
      <para>
        <link url="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PF8fRA107OA;&amp;rel=0">View the instructional video</link>
        and
        <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cd42f6dfe23e48d50c003bbdc11d758914a4acf0">follow along with the materials</link>
        to assist you with learning this routine.
      </para>
      <para>Provide support for students</para>
      <para>
        <link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dd41dd1a4057402964bff003365e7e89158db868">Distribute graphic organizers</link>
           to the students to assist them with participating in this routine.
      </para>
</note>

<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Pattern 1</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Pattern. Step 0,1 dot. Step 1, 3 dots, 2 by 2 with upper left dot removed. Step 2, 5 dots, 3 by 2 with upper left dot removed. Step 3, 7 dots, 4 by 2 with upper left dot removed."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d40bf31a833606cb35897e28384bce0fad6c41e6"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Pattern 2</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Pattern. Step 0, 0 dots. Step 1, 1 dot. Step 2, 4 dots, arranged 2 by 2. Step 3, 9 dots, arranged 3 by 3."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/32e3f0ae641cc7113cbd7ee68d29febdea28d604"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Look at Pattern 1.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> How is the number of dots in each step changing? 
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Each step has 2 rows of dots, with each row having the same number of dots as the step
      number, plus one extra dot.
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> How many dots will be in Step 5 in the pattern, and how will they be arranged? 
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> In Step 5, there will be 2 rows of 5 dots plus one additional dot.
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Look at Pattern 2.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> How is the number of dots in each step changing? 
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Each step has as many rows and columns of dots as the step number.
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> How many dots will be in Step 5 in the pattern, and how will they be arranged? 
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Step 5 is a 5-by-5 array of dots.
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para>Consider the following table.</para>
<table class="os-raise-wideadjustedtable">
  <caption/>
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        Step
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        Number of dots in Pattern 1
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        Number of dots in Pattern 2
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        0
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        7
      </entry>
      <entry>
        9
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        10
      </entry>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        12
      </entry>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(n\)
      </entry>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Complete the table with the number of dots for Pattern 1.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> In Step 4, what is the number of dots in Pattern 1?
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 9
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> In Step 5, what is the number of dots in Pattern 1?
      
      <emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>11
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> In Step 10, what is the number of dots in Pattern 1?
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 21
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
    <item> In Step 12, what is the number of dots in Pattern 1?
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 25
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
    <item> In Step \(n\), what is the number of dots in Pattern 1? 
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(2n+1\)
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Complete the table with the number of dots for Pattern 2.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> In Step 4, what is the number of dots in Pattern 2? 
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 16
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> In Step 5, what is the number of dots in Pattern 2?
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 25
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> In Step 10, what is the number of dots in Pattern 2?
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 100
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
    <item> In Step 12, what is the number of dots in Pattern 2?
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> 144
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
    <item> In Step \(n\), what is the number of dots in Pattern 2?
      
      <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(n \cdot n\) or \(n^2\)
    </item>
  </list>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. Plot the number of dots at each step number for Pattern 1
    and Pattern 2.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> Pattern 1 </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para>Select the<emphasis> solution </emphasis>button to compare your work.</para>
<para> Answer:</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Points plotted on a grid. Number of dots. Step number."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/cf515b348bca3de8f3b02eb7c8f2deca3dccc3e7"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> Pattern 2 </item>
  </list>
</list>
<para>Select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
<para>Answer:</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Points plotted on a grid. Number of dots. Step number."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/bb98bf2467269ae301fca6f978cc21315c4ae42b"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Explain why the graphs of the two patterns look the way they do.
    
    <emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The points representing the first pattern lie on a line because getting from one step to
    the next involves adding the same number of dots (2 dots). The points representing the second pattern do not lie on
    a line. They “curve” upward because the number of dots added at each step increases from step to step.
  </item>
</list>
<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite students to describe how they see the number of dots change in each pattern. Focus the discussion on the
  connections between students’ descriptions, the changes in the table, and the graphs. Make sure these points are
  uncovered in the discussion:</para>
<list>
  <item> For the first pattern, each successive step adds two dots. The growth is constant. The points on the graph form a
    straight line. </item>
  <item> For the second pattern, the number of dots that is added changes (and grows) at each step. As a result, the
    number of dots for the second pattern curves upward when graphed. </item>
</list>
<para>Highlight that, in the second pattern, the expression for the number of dots at Step \(n\) can be expressed as
  \(n^2\), which can be read as “\(n\) squared.” Point out that the “squaring” is also evident
  in the visual pattern.</para>
<title>7.2.2: Self Check</title>
<para><emphasis>After the activity, students will answer the following question to check their
    understanding of the concepts explored in the activity.</emphasis></para>
<para>QUESTION:</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/90fb987a737979a109a5022cbec5896a09182c17"/></media></figure></para>
<para>How many squares will be in Step 5?</para>

<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
  <caption/>
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        Answers
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        Feedback
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        20
      </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: This is the number of squares in Step 4. The answer is 30.
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        25
      </entry>
      <entry>
        <para>Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The number of rows is the same as the number of the step,
          and the number of columns is 1 more than the number of the step. The answer is 30.
      </para></entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        30
      </entry>
      <entry>
        That’s correct! Check yourself: The number of rows is the same as the number of the step, and the number
        of columns is 1 more than the number of the step. Step 5 has 5 rows and 6 columns.
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        35 </entry>
      <entry>
        Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: The number of rows is the same as the number of the step, and
        the number of columns is 1 more than the number of the step. The answer is 30.
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>7.2.2: Additional Resources</title>
<para><emphasis>The following content is available to students who would like more support based on
    their experience with the self check. Students will not automatically have access to this content, so you may wish
    to share it with those who could benefit from it.</emphasis></para>
<title>Writing Expressions for Patterns</title>
<para>Example 1</para>
<para><figure><media alt="DIAGRAM WITH 3 FIGURES. STEP 1 IS SHAPED LIKE A PLUS SIGN, WITH 1 SQUARE IN THE MIDDLE AND 1 SQUARE ATTACHED TO EACH OF ITS SIDES. STEP 2 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 2 ROWS AND 4 COLUMNS. ABOVE AND BELOW THE SECOND COLUMN OF THE RECTANGLE IS 1 SQUARE. STEP 3 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 3 ROWS AND 5 COLUMNS. ABOVE AND BELOW THE SECOND COLUMN OF THE RECTANGLE IS 1 SQUARE.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/0a1a836e44bf7cd832a0d520642fc03dad9e66c3"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Find the number of squares in Step 5:</para>
<para>First, look to see the pattern. Every step, another row and another column are added, plus the two extra squares.</para>
<list>
  <item> Step 1: 1 row of \(3 + 2\) </item>
  <item> Step 2: 2 rows of \(4 +2\) </item>
  <item> Step 3: 3 rows of \(5 + 2\) </item>
</list>
<para>So the width is always 1 by \(n\), or \(n\). The length is \(n+2\), and then another 2 is added.</para>
<para>Then, draw pictures or create a table:</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        Step
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        # of squares
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        10
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        17
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        26
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
      <entry>
        37
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>At Step 5, there are 37 squares.</para>
<para>Example 2</para>
<para>Find the expression for Step \(n\).</para>
<para>Multiply the length and width, and then add the 2 extra squares:</para>
<para>\(n(n+2)+2 = n^2+2n+n\)</para>
<title>Try It: Writing Expressions for Patterns</title>
<para>Use the figure below to find the number of squares in Step 5 and then Step \(n\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="DIAGRAM WITH 3 FIGURES. STEP 1 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 2 ROWS AND 3 COLUMNS. 2 SQUARES OF A THIRD ROW ARE ADDED. STEP 2 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 3 ROWS AND 4 COLUMNS. 3 SQUARES OF A FOURTH ROW ARE ADDED. STEP 3 HAS A RECTANGLE WITH 4 ROWS AND 5 COLUMNS. 4 SQUARES OF A FIFTH ROW ARE ADDED.&#10;"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/1b6a05a9afdaeddde371b9f9242292998e4cc777"/></media></figure></para>
<para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution </emphasis>button to compare your work.</para>
<title>Solution</title>
<para>Here is how to determine the number of squares in each step:</para>
<para>Notice that each figure adds a row and column, and then subtracts one square. The number of rows is always 2 more
  than the step number.</para>
<para>Begin by making a table:</para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis>
        Step
      </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis>
        # of squares
      </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        1
      </entry>
      <entry>
        8
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        2
      </entry>
      <entry>
        15
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        3
      </entry>
      <entry>
        24
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        4
      </entry>
      <entry>
        35
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        5
      </entry>
      <entry>
        48
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>There are 48 squares in Step 5.</para>
<para>Step \(n\) would be found with \((n+2)(n+2)-1\) or \((n+2)^2-1\).</para></section>
